# T29 — Phase 4 — Hybrid candidate retrieval (graph + docs + memories + events + working set)

**Phase:** 4
**Type:** backend
**Model class:** advanced
**Depends on:** T27, T28
**Spec anchor:** [§Phase 4: Retrieval V1](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-4-retrieval-v1), [§Retrieval Engine](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#7-retrieval-engine)
**Standards:** Cadres coding standard (`/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md`); Lattice project rules (`/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`).

## Goal

This task delivers pipeline steps 4 through 7 from spec §Retrieval Engine: gather candidate results from every source enumerated under "Candidate sources" — exact path/symbol lookup, code graph traversal, doc backlinks/outgoing links, FTS, embeddings, event similarity, memory links, workflow similarity, recent active working memory — and produce a unified `Candidate` stream the scorer (T30) can rank. Each candidate carries its source, the resolved anchor(s) that produced it, and a preliminary `inclusion_reason` string the shaper (T31) will refine. Hot-path budgets from spec §Phase 1/§Phase 2 still apply, so per-source retrieval is bounded by configurable caps and never performs unbounded traversal.

## Spec excerpts that drive this task

> "Pipeline: ... 4. Retrieve graph candidates. 5. Retrieve memory candidates from typed streams. 6. Retrieve relevant event episodes. 7. Expand through bounded graph and memory neighborhoods." (spec §7. Retrieval Engine)

> "Candidate sources: exact path/symbol lookup; code graph traversal; doc backlinks/outgoing links; FTS; embeddings; event similarity; memory links; workflow similarity; recent active working memory." (spec §7. Retrieval Engine)

> "Phase 4: Retrieval V1 — Deliverables: ... hybrid candidate retrieval from graph, docs, memories, and events ..." (spec §Phase 4: Retrieval V1)

> "No unbounded payload growth, graph traversal, or event-log scan on hot paths." (spec §Non-Negotiable Product Properties)

## Inputs

- Dependency outputs:
  - T27 `IntentClassification` — selects which candidate sources are weighted/required for the active intent.
  - T28 `ResolvedAnchor` stream — provides the seed identities for exact lookup, graph traversal, and doc backlink expansion.
- Existing files in the repo this task builds on:
  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/graph/` — graph traversal entry points.
  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/storage/` — FTS (`memories_fts`, planned doc FTS) and embedding index access.
  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/embeddings/` and `.lattice/vector_index/` USearch handle — embedding nearest-neighbour lookup.
  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/events/` (Phase 2 deliverable) — event similarity / episode lookup APIs.
  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory/` — typed memory streams + link tables (Phase 3 deliverables).
- Standards files to honor: `/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md` (file ≤800 lines, function ≤50 lines, single source of truth — reuse existing search APIs); `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md` (no broken windows; hot-path budgets are explicit).

## Steps

1. Re-read spec §Retrieval Engine "Candidate sources" and "Pipeline" steps 4–7 in full; note the explicit ordering between exact lookup → graph traversal → docs → FTS/embeddings → events → memory links → workflow → working memory.
2. Define `Candidate { identity, source, seed_anchor, raw_score, preliminary_inclusion_reason, expansion_handle_hint }` in `candidates.rs`. The `source` variant enumerates exactly the nine spec-named sources; no additional sources sneak in without spec amendment.
3. Implement one async retriever per source as a small dedicated function (≤50 lines each), gated by a per-source budget cap drawn from a `RetrievalBudget` struct (max candidates per source, max graph hops, max FTS rows, embedding k, event window, working-memory window). Defaults must come from configuration, not literals scattered across functions.
4. Compose the retrievers in `retrieve_candidates(intent: &IntentClassification, anchors: &[ResolvedAnchor], budget: &RetrievalBudget, ctx: &RetrievalContext) -> Vec<Candidate>` so the orchestration is one place, parallel via `tokio::join!` where safe, and short-circuits when intent disqualifies a source (e.g. `update_docs` skips event similarity unless explicitly requested).
5. Wire bounded graph expansion (spec pipeline step 7) through the existing graph-traversal API — never call `petgraph` directly here. Expansion radius is `min(intent_default, budget.max_graph_hops)` and every traversal must record the path that produced the candidate (used by T30 graph-proximity scoring and by T31's expansion handle).
6. Hot-path discipline: log the candidate count per source and total elapsed time at `debug` (never `info` on hot paths). Assert in debug builds that no single retriever exceeded its budget cap; in release builds, truncate with a `BudgetExhausted` flag on the candidate set so the scorer/shaper can surface it.
7. Add `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/retrieval_v1/candidates_tests.rs` covering: each of the nine sources produces candidates for an appropriate seed; budgets are enforced (synthetic huge graph does not blow up); intent gating skips disqualified sources; bounded expansion records traversal paths; FTS, embeddings, and event similarity all participate when present; working-memory window returns the most-recent N items from the session context (interface stub acceptable here — T35 wires the real producer); empty anchor list still yields working-memory and event-similarity results when those exist.
8. Run `cd daemon && cargo build --release` and the verification test command; fix any clippy warnings before stopping.

## Files expected

- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/retrieval_v1/candidates.rs` — `Candidate` type, per-source retrievers, orchestrator, budget enforcement.
- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/retrieval_v1/candidates_tests.rs` — in-tree tests for source coverage, budgets, intent gating, bounded expansion, traversal-path recording.

## Verification

- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/retrieval_v1/candidates.rs` — confirms the candidates module exists at the expected path.
- `cd daemon && cargo test -p lattice-core --lib retrieval_v1::candidates_tests` — runs the in-tree tests and asserts every spec-named source has at least one passing test.

## Coding-standard checks

- File length ≤800 lines per file. If `candidates.rs` approaches the cap, split per-source retrievers into `candidates/` submodules along source boundaries — never along arbitrary line counts.
- Function length ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, cyclomatic ≤10. Each retriever is its own function; the orchestrator is composition, not a giant match.
- Schema parity: not applicable (no SQL in this task).
- No `TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX`, commented-out code, or unjustified `#[allow(...)]`.
- Single source of truth: graph traversal, FTS, and embedding queries are issued through their existing module APIs; no duplicated SQL or vector-index calls in this module.
- No broken windows: dead imports removed, every public name expresses intent; hot-path log levels are correct (`debug`, not `info`).
- Doc citation: comments naming the spec-defined source list cite `## 7. Retrieval Engine` per `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`.

## Definition of done

- [ ] Every file in "Files expected" exists with real content (no stubs).
- [ ] Every verification command in tasks.json passes from repo root.
- [ ] Coding-standard checks satisfied.
- [ ] No deferred work: every one of the nine spec-named candidate sources has a retriever and a test.
- [ ] All retrievals are budget-bounded; no unbounded traversal or FTS scan on the hot path.
- [ ] Candidates expose `source`, `seed_anchor`, and `expansion_handle_hint`, ready to be consumed by T30 (scoring) and T31 (shaper).

